# Managed by Ansible (roles/core_service) — local edits are overwritten.
#
# The one privilege a deploy needs. deploy/deploy.sh restarts core after flipping the
# release symlinks; everything else it does — unpacking, writing config.json, pruning old releases —
# happens as the deploy account's own user under /srv/easy.
#
# Two exact commands, no wildcards. `systemctl` with a free argument would be equivalent to root,
# since it can start a unit the caller is able to write, and `journalctl` with a free argument reads
# every log on the host. Spelling out the exact invocations the script uses costs nothing and means
# neither is a general grant.
#
# The journalctl line exists because the script fetches core's log when a deploy fails. Without the
# grant that prompts for a password, the prompt cannot be answered non-interactively, and the error
# is swallowed — so a failed deploy would print no diagnostics at exactly the moment they matter.
#
# `easy-core-log` is the same privilege for a wider time window, and it is a script rather than a
# third journalctl line for a reason worth knowing: journalctl accepts `-u` repeatedly and ORs the
# units, and a sudoers wildcard matches the entire rest of the command line — so
# `journalctl -u {{ easy_core_service_name }} *` would also permit `... -u ssh`, i.e. the auth log.
# The unit can only be pinned outside sudoers. The script is root-owned and must stay unwritable by
# these accounts, or the grant becomes equivalent to root.
#
# The fixed `-n 40` line stays because deploy/deploy.sh spells exactly that, and a deploy failing
# for want of a sudo password is the failure this whole file exists to prevent.
#
# The dump line is what lets a deploy take a restore point before it restarts core, which production
# does on every deploy: Liquibase migrations are forward-only, so the dump is the only way back
# across one. `start` and not `restart` or `stop`, and the unit is spelled out — a systemctl grant
# with a free argument is equivalent to root, because it can start a unit the caller can write.
# Starting a backup is not a privilege in any other sense: the unit runs as postgres, writes one
# directory, and cannot reach the network.
{% for user in easy_core_deploy_users %}
{{ user }} ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart {{ easy_core_service_name }}
{{ user }} ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/journalctl -u {{ easy_core_service_name }} -n 40 --no-pager
{{ user }} ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: {{ easy_core_log_helper_path }}
{{ user }} ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: {{ easy_core_log_helper_path }} *
{{ user }} ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start {{ easy_core_deploy_dump_service }}
{% endfor %}
